WEBVTT

00:00.614 --> 00:02.851
- [Narrator] We're told that
the double number line shows

00:02.851 --> 00:05.934
that five pounds of avocados cost $9.

00:07.836 --> 00:10.452
And so what is going on here
with this double number line?

00:10.452 --> 00:14.640
This shows how as we increase
the number of avocados,

00:14.640 --> 00:16.583
how the cost increases.

00:16.583 --> 00:19.875
So for example, when we have
zero pounds of avocados,

00:19.875 --> 00:21.763
it costs us $0.

00:21.763 --> 00:24.492
When we have five pounds of avocados,

00:24.492 --> 00:25.742
it costs us $9.

00:27.570 --> 00:29.537
And so, if you look at
any point over here,

00:29.537 --> 00:30.855
let's say you look right over here.

00:30.855 --> 00:32.910
This would be, let's
see, this is one, right?

00:32.910 --> 00:34.910
This is one, two, three, four, five.

00:34.910 --> 00:38.512
If you look at one, this
point on the cost number line

00:38.512 --> 00:43.131
would tell you how much one
pound of avocado would cost.

00:43.131 --> 00:45.632
Two pounds of avocados,
how much would that cost?

00:45.632 --> 00:48.590
You would look at this second
number line right over there.

00:48.590 --> 00:51.923
So they ask us, based on the ratio shown

00:54.195 --> 00:57.257
in the double number
line, what is the cost

00:57.257 --> 00:59.424
for one pound of avocados?

01:00.641 --> 01:02.240
So pause the video and think about it.

01:02.240 --> 01:05.152
Remember, one pound of avocados
on this top number line,

01:05.152 --> 01:06.633
we look at this second number line,

01:06.633 --> 01:08.049
the cost would be right over here.

01:08.049 --> 01:10.132
What is this going to be?

01:11.863 --> 01:14.380
Well, we could just set it up as a ratio.

01:14.380 --> 01:17.146
The ratio of pounds of avocados to cost

01:17.146 --> 01:20.621
is going to be, let me
do this in some colors.

01:20.621 --> 01:23.704
So if I have five pounds of avocados,

01:24.941 --> 01:27.108
it is going to cost me $9.

01:28.061 --> 01:31.490
So the ratio of pounds to
dollars is five to nine.

01:31.490 --> 01:35.111
So if I were to have one pound of avocado,

01:35.111 --> 01:37.358
one pound of avocado,

01:37.358 --> 01:42.103
I have divided by five to
get one pound of avocado.

01:42.103 --> 01:44.803
I would have to do the
same thing for the cost.

01:44.803 --> 01:47.136
So if I divide nine by five,

01:48.469 --> 01:52.523
this is going to be nine fifths dollars.

01:52.523 --> 01:55.581
Nine fifths dollars would
be the cost of one pound.

01:55.581 --> 01:57.567
Well, nine fifths isn't
always the most natural way

01:57.567 --> 02:01.930
to write money, so you
can view this nine fifths

02:01.930 --> 02:05.017
is equal to one and four fifths,

02:05.017 --> 02:07.708
which is equal to one and eight 10ths,

02:07.708 --> 02:09.934
which is equal to 1.8,

02:09.934 --> 02:12.517
or you could say this is $1.80.

02:13.786 --> 02:15.750
So if you were to go onto
this double number line,

02:15.750 --> 02:18.258
the cost of one pound of avocado,

02:18.258 --> 02:21.729
this point right over here would be $1.80.

02:21.729 --> 02:23.199
If you said two pounds of avocados,

02:23.199 --> 02:24.819
well, now you would double it.

02:24.819 --> 02:26.736
So this would be $3.60,

02:28.203 --> 02:30.043
and you would go on and on and on

02:30.043 --> 02:33.618
all the way until you got to $9 here.

02:33.618 --> 02:35.701
Let's do another example.

02:36.880 --> 02:40.604
So here, we are told the
double number line shows

02:40.604 --> 02:44.646
how many model trains
Irene can build in a week.

02:44.646 --> 02:47.802
So we can see, in zero weeks
she can't build any trains,

02:47.802 --> 02:50.978
but in one week she can build nine trains.

02:50.978 --> 02:53.865
And they asked us, which
table represents the rate

02:53.865 --> 02:56.448
of Irene building model trains?

02:57.606 --> 03:00.949
So pause this video and see
if you can figure it out.

03:00.949 --> 03:04.160
So once again, every week
she can build nine trains.

03:04.160 --> 03:05.931
So one way to think about it is

03:05.931 --> 03:08.568
the ratio of week to trains

03:08.568 --> 03:10.256
would be one

03:10.256 --> 03:11.089
to nine.

03:12.451 --> 03:13.455
So let's see.

03:13.455 --> 03:15.925
If I look at this table, I
just wanna see where the ratio

03:15.925 --> 03:19.546
between weeks to trains
stays at one to nine.

03:19.546 --> 03:23.330
So five to 45, that is still one to nine.

03:23.330 --> 03:27.395
To go from one to five,
I've multiplied by five.

03:27.395 --> 03:32.218
And then to go to nine to 45,
I've also multiplied by five.

03:32.218 --> 03:34.423
So this one checks out.

03:34.423 --> 03:38.961
And another way to think about
it, is 45 is nine times five.

03:38.961 --> 03:40.782
So nine times.

03:40.782 --> 03:42.946
That might be an easier
way to think about it.

03:42.946 --> 03:46.307
Over here, 12 to 108, that's, once again,

03:46.307 --> 03:48.057
12 times nine is 108.

03:49.325 --> 03:50.908
And then 26 to 234.

03:52.280 --> 03:56.332
Let's see, 26 times 10
would be 260, minus 26.

03:56.332 --> 03:58.126
Yeah, it would be 234.

03:58.126 --> 03:59.482
So this is nine times.

03:59.482 --> 04:02.199
So in all of these cases,
the ratio of trains

04:02.199 --> 04:06.130
or the ratio of weeks to
trains is one to nine.

04:06.130 --> 04:08.938
So this one is looking good,
so I'll just circle that in.

04:08.938 --> 04:11.188
But let's just make sure
that this one doesn't work.

04:11.188 --> 04:15.960
Over here, the ratio of weeks
to trains is nine to one,

04:15.960 --> 04:17.989
not one to nine.

04:17.989 --> 04:20.761
The train should be nine times the weeks,

04:20.761 --> 04:23.106
while here the weeks is
nine times the train.

04:23.106 --> 04:24.378
So just looking at that first one,

04:24.378 --> 04:28.002
we know that this is
not going to work out.

04:28.002 --> 04:30.169
Let's do one last example.

04:31.447 --> 04:35.217
The double number line shows
how many snowballs Jacob

04:35.217 --> 04:37.719
and his friends can make in one minute.

04:37.719 --> 04:40.137
No minutes, they can make zero snowballs.

04:40.137 --> 04:42.328
In one minute, they can make 12.

04:42.328 --> 04:44.699
Complete the table to
show the same information

04:44.699 --> 04:47.108
as the double number line.

04:47.108 --> 04:48.202
So once again, pause this video

04:48.202 --> 04:50.711
and see if you can work this out.

04:50.711 --> 04:53.339
Well, we can think about it as a ratio.

04:53.339 --> 04:55.776
The ratio of minutes

04:55.776 --> 04:57.943
to snowballs is one to 12.

04:59.583 --> 05:02.666
So minutes to snowballs is one to 12.

05:03.967 --> 05:05.438
Or another way to think about it is,

05:05.438 --> 05:09.637
the snowballs is going to
be 12 times the minutes.

05:09.637 --> 05:12.763
And so over here, if I have 12 snowballs,

05:12.763 --> 05:16.107
we already know that's
going to be one minute.

05:16.107 --> 05:19.390
If I have 48 snowballs,
let's just think about it.

05:19.390 --> 05:22.540
To go from 12 to 48 you
have to multiply by four.

05:22.540 --> 05:23.991
So you have four times as many snowballs,

05:23.991 --> 05:27.969
it's gonna take you four
times as many minutes.

05:27.969 --> 05:30.923
And then if you go to five,
to go from one to five

05:30.923 --> 05:34.479
you multiply by five, so
you're gonna have five times

05:34.479 --> 05:37.083
as many snowballs as you
would be able to make

05:37.083 --> 05:37.987
in one minute.

05:37.987 --> 05:41.369
So five times 12 is 60, and we're done.

05:41.369 --> 05:45.202
It could be one to 12,
four to 48, five to 60.

05:46.271 --> 05:50.703
For every five minutes,
you can make 60 snowballs,

05:50.703 --> 05:52.243
or they make 60 snowballs.
